,"collectionIntroductions":{"american":{"heading":"American collection (25)","text":"These 25 expressions are grouped by the situations an American adult in Spain actually meets them in. 7 have a full DLE reading, 2 a partial one, 14 are attested in other sources, 1 is a gloss and 1 is natural usage still to confirm. Many are shared with Canadian English; the Canadian section covers the distinctly Canadian ones."},"canadian":{"heading":"Canadian collection (25)","text":"These 25 are distinctly Canadian, or used in Canada in ways a Spanish speaker will not expect, so many have no Spanish idiom and need a short gloss. A dictionary labels 18 of them Canadian: keener, snowbird, washroom, give’r (only its “party hard” sense carries a regional label, Western Canada), gong show, double-double, loonie and toonie, toque, cottage country, hydro, chesterfield, two-four, eh, hoser, dépanneur, garburator, eavestrough and kerfuffle (Canadian Oxford Dictionary “Cdn, Brit., & Austral. informal”, as cited by the Dictionary of Canadianisms). The other 7 are Canadian by usage or stereotype, with no Canadian dictionary label found: pitch in, not bad, sorry, Canadian tuxedo (the Dictionary of Canadianisms records it as emerging in Canada around 2000, but Dictionary.com labels it American), mickey, pop and take a kick at the can. “Hang a Larry” was dropped because the Dictionary of Canadianisms defines it as a left turn, not a U-turn, and “beauty!” because no dictionary labels it Canadian."}},"statusDefinitions":[{"status":"DLE","definition":"the exact Spanish phrase was read on dle.rae.es in this check, with its marking (coloq., loc. verb.). “DLE (partial)” means only a related sense or example was read."},{"status":"Attested","definition":"not confirmed in the DLE here, but listed in a bilingual dictionary (SpanishDict, WordReference, Larousse), Wiktionary or Spain-based press. Named in the status cell."},{"status":"Usage","definition":"natural Peninsular Spanish from my own knowledge; no source I could read confirmed it. Check before you publish it as verified."},{"status":"Gloss","definition":"Spanish has no fixed idiom, so the rendering explains the meaning rather than translating the phrase."},{"status":"Avoid","definition":"a literal calque that people in Spain would not say; shown so learners recognise the trap."}],"overview":"No entry is checked against the RAE alone: where a DLE page would not load, the rendering was tested against bilingual dictionaries, Fundéu, Spain-based press and, for the English side, the Dictionary of Canadianisms (DCHP) and other dictionaries.","sourceLimitations":{"heading":"How far to trust the sources.","text":"SpanishDict, WordReference and Larousse show that a phrase is in use, not that the RAE endorses it, and SpanishDict’s regional tags are sometimes wrong. Fundéu’s preference for “bandera roja” was read through an EFE reprint, not on Fundéu’s own site. Pages were read through a summarising fetch tool, so quotes are as it reported them."}}
